.\" Template. The Makefile renders this into build/<command>.1 by substituting
.\" the @PLACEHOLDERS@ from src/core/branding.py — edit this file, never the
.\" generated one, and never write the project's name here.
.TH @COMMAND_UPPER@ 1 "@DATE@" "@NAME@ @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
@COMMAND@ \- @DESCRIPTION@
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B @COMMAND@
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B @NAME@
turns interview practice into timed, scored, permanently recorded runs.
You solve problems in a browser and report the verdict yourself;
.B @NAME@
times the attempt, records what happened, and prompts you to write down what
made it hard while you still remember.
.PP
Everything is stored as an append-only event log. Run summaries and rankings
are projections rebuilt from that log, and scores are computed when you read
them rather than stored, so changing the scoring weights re-scores all of your
history at once. Nothing is ever overwritten.
.PP
With no command,
.B @COMMAND@
starts the terminal interface and lands on the home screen.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B stats
Solve times as distributions rather than averages: the 50th, 90th and 99th
percentiles, what is dragging the tail, and how the current window compares to
the one before it. The 99th percentile is greyed out on small samples, where it
is one attempt and not a statistic.
.TP
.B history
Every run, ranked against every other run.
.TP
.B queue
Today's queue: which problems to do, in what order, and why. Reviews are
scheduled by FSRS from your own history and lead the queue, but never take more
than about 40% of it, so new coverage does not stall. No problem attempted in
the last three days appears unless it has come due, the difficulty mix is
targeted at roughly 20/60/20, and no pattern ever runs three problems deep:
interleaving beats blocking for transfer. Generated on first use each day.
.TP
.B replay
Rebuild all projections from the event log. Safe to run at any time; it changes
no history, only the tables derived from it.
.TP
.B seed
Insert or update the problem catalog. Never touches attempt history.
.TP
.B fetch
Cache the active list's problem statements for offline use, so a flight is a
normal run. Downloads the whole list in priority order — today's queue first,
then cards by due date, then the rest spread across patterns — until
.I cache.max_mb
is spent. Self-contained pages with images inlined; nothing is left to request.
Idempotent and resumable, and a problem that will not download is reported
rather than ending the sweep. Set
.I cache.offline
to point
.B o
at the cached copy instead of the browser.
.TP
.B doctor
Print the resolved paths, catalog size, editor, cache state and configuration.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS stats
.TP
.BI \-\-tag " TAG"
Restrict to problems carrying a tag.
.TP
.BI \-\-pattern " PATTERN"
Restrict to one pattern group, e.g.
.IR sliding-window .
.TP
.BI \-\-difficulty " easy|medium|hard"
Restrict to one difficulty.
.TP
.BI \-\-days " N"
Look back N days.
.B 0
means all time.
.TP
.BI \-\-by " pattern|difficulty|tag"
Also print one distribution per group, ordered by volume.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Maximum number of groups to print with
.BR \-\-by .
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-samples " N"
Sample count below which the 99th percentile is greyed out.
.SS queue
.TP
.BI \-n " N"
Problems in the queue. Defaults to
.I session.planned_n
from the configuration.
.TP
.B \-\-regenerate
Rebuild today's queue from the current cards, replacing the one already stored
for today.
.SS history
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Show only the most recent N runs.
.TP
.BI \-\-run " N"
Expand one run, by run number, into its per-problem breakdown.
.SS seed
.TP
.BI \-\-file " PATH"
Seed from a JSON catalog instead of the bundled one.
.TP
.BI \-\-list " NAME"
Which bundled catalog to seed.
.SS fetch
.TP
.B \-\-refresh
Re-download problems already cached.
.TP
.BI \-\-list " NAME"
Cache this list instead of the active one.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-mb " N"
Cache size ceiling, overriding
.IR cache.max_mb .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the priority order and what is already cached. Fetches nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-session
Prompt for your
.B LEETCODE_SESSION
cookie and store it at
.I ~/.config/@SLUG@/session
with mode 0600, then continue. Prompted rather than taken as an argument so it
never appears in
.BR ps (1)
or your shell history.
.SH KEYS
Navigation is vim's, on every screen. The arrow keys, Home/End and
PageUp/PageDown work everywhere too.
.TP
.BR j ", " k
Down, up.
.TP
.BR h ", " l
Left, right. Where a screen has two panes, these move between them.
.TP
.BR gg ", " G
Top, bottom.
.TP
.BR ^d ", " ^u
Half a screen down, up.
.TP
.BR ^f ", " ^b
A full screen down, up.
.PP
Because those keys are motions everywhere, no screen binds them to an action.
.PP
On the home screen:
.B n
starts a run,
.B d
opens today's queue,
.B r
opens the runs,
.B t
opens the statistics,
.B s
opens the settings,
.B q
quits. The menu is also a list, so
.BR j / k
and Enter reach the same places.
.PP
On the queue screen:
.B Enter
(or
.BR ^s )
starts a run over the queue as it stands,
.B ^r
rebuilds it from the current cards, and
.B q
goes back.
.PP
On the setup screen:
.B /
(or
.BR i )
moves to the filter box and
.B Escape
comes back out of it to the list — a second
.B Escape
leaves the screen.
.B Space
picks a problem,
.B ^r
rolls a fresh random set,
.B ^s
starts the run.
.PP
On the active problem screen:
.TP
.B o
Open the problem in a browser.
.TP
.B p
Pause and resume. Paused time is recorded, not hidden.
.TP
.B ?
Reveal the next hint tier. Hints are monotonic and cannot be taken back; the
last tier is the full solution and ends the attempt.
.TP
.B s
Log a failed submit, then hand you an editor buffer to paste the code that was
rejected. Skippable with
.BR :q! ,
like every capture step, and switchable off in the settings screen.
.TP
.B f
Finish: verdict, self-confidence, then the two editor steps.
.TP
.B x
Give up. Scores zero, and is still recorded.
.TP
.B q
End the run.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/@SLUG@/config.toml
Written on first run with every default present and commented. The settings
screen never rewrites it: changes made there are events layered on top, and
.B x
on a settings row drops the override and takes this file's value back.
.TP
.I ~/.config/@SLUG@/session
Your LeetCode session cookie, if you have one, mode 0600. Optional: without it
premium problems cannot be cached. Kept out of both
.I config.toml
and the settings table on purpose — the settings table is a projection of an
append-only log, so a credential written there could never be removed and would
survive every replay.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/@SLUG@/@SLUG@.db
The event log and the projections built from it.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/@SLUG@/cache/<problem>.html
Cached problem statements, self-contained. Derived and disposable: deleting the
directory costs nothing but a re-fetch.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/@SLUG@/code/<problem>/<attempt-id>.<ext>
Archived solutions.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/@SLUG@/code/<problem>/<attempt-id>-wrong<n>.<ext>
Archived failed submits, beside the solution they became.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/@SLUG@/notes/<problem>/<attempt-id>.md
Reflection notes.
.PP
Solutions and notes are kept as ordinary files rather than in the database so
they can be searched, diffed and opened with the usual tools.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B @ENVPFX@_HOME
Relocate the configuration and data directories under one path. Useful for a
throwaway profile.
.TP
.B @ENVPFX@_DB
Use a specific database file.
.TP
.B @ENVPFX@_EDITOR
Editor for the capture steps. Takes precedence over
.B VISUAL
and
.BR EDITOR .
.TP
.B @ENVPFX@_BROWSER
Command used to open a problem.
.TP
.B @ENVPFX@_LEETCODE_SESSION
Your
.B LEETCODE_SESSION
cookie, unlocking premium problems for
.BR fetch .
Takes precedence over the
.I session
file. Never printed, never logged, and never written into a cached page.
.TP
.BR VISUAL ", " EDITOR
Consulted, in that order, when
.B @ENVPFX@_EDITOR
is unset. Without any of them the capture steps are skipped, which costs
nothing but loses the notes.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME
Respected when
.B @ENVPFX@_HOME
is unset.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 2
The command line could not be parsed.
.SH NOTES
Notes are collected from the first run even though nothing reads them yet.
Measurements can be recomputed from the event log forever; reflections cannot
be written after the fact.
